WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.839
The next portion of model context protocol that we're going to investigate is the client.

2
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:09.199
The purpose of the client is to provide a means of communication between your server and a MCP

3
00:00:09.199 --> 00:00:13.680
server. This client is going to be your access point to all the tools implemented by that server.

4
00:00:14.239 --> 00:00:19.040
Now MCP is transport agnostic. This is a fancy term that just says that the

5
00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:23.120
client and the server can communicate over a variety of different protocols.

6
00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:29.520
A very common way to run a MCP server right now is on the same physical machine as the MCP client.

7
00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:32.799
And if these two things are running on the same machine then they can communicate over

8
00:00:32.799 --> 00:00:37.520
standard input output. And that's what we are going to be setting up later on inside this section.

9
00:00:38.159 --> 00:00:43.520
There are however other ways we can connect the MCP client with the MCP server. So they can also

10
00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:49.200
connect over HTTP or WebSockets or any of a number of other varieties or techniques.

11
00:00:50.159 --> 00:00:53.279
Once a connection has been formed between the client and the server,

12
00:00:53.279 --> 00:00:58.000
they communicate by exchanging messages. The exact messages that are allowed are all

13
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:02.880
defined inside of the MCP spec. Some of the message types that you and I are going to be

14
00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:09.839
focusing on are the ListToolsRequest and the ListToolsResult. As you guessed, ListToolsRequest

15
00:01:09.839 --> 00:01:13.199
is sent from the client to the server, and it asks the server to list out all the different

16
00:01:13.199 --> 00:01:18.160
tools that it provides. The server would then respond with a ListToolsResult message,

17
00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:21.839
which contains a list of all the different tools that it can provide.

18
00:01:21.839 --> 00:01:26.080
Two other common message types that you and I are going to see are the CallToolRequest

19
00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:31.279
and CallToolResult. The first will ask the server to run a tool with some particular arguments,

20
00:01:31.279 --> 00:01:34.239
and the second will contain the result of the tool run.

21
00:01:35.199 --> 00:01:39.760
Now at this point in time, we've got this idea of a server and a client,

22
00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:44.239
but I suspect it's probably not really clear how all this stuff really works together.

23
00:01:44.239 --> 00:01:48.480
So here's what we're going to do in the remainder of this video. We are going to walk through

24
00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:53.599
an example call between a lot of different things. So this will be kind of an involved process,

25
00:01:53.599 --> 00:01:57.839
but we're going to imagine the communication that goes on between a user, our server that

26
00:01:57.839 --> 00:02:02.800
we're putting together, a MCP client, a MCP server, GitHub as some provider that we're

27
00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:07.839
trying to access some data from, and Cloud. So let's get to it. Again, step by step.

28
00:02:07.839 --> 00:02:11.679
First thing we would expect to happen is a user to submit some kind of query or question to our

29
00:02:11.679 --> 00:02:16.960
server, like what repositories do I have? At this point, it would be up to our server to make a

30
00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:21.759
request off to Cloud. But in that request, we want to list out all the different tools that Cloud has

31
00:02:21.759 --> 00:02:25.919
access to. So before our server can make the request off to Cloud, it's first going to go

32
00:02:25.919 --> 00:02:30.559
through a little side detour through the MCP client and the server. So here's what happens.

33
00:02:31.199 --> 00:02:35.919
The server is going to realize that it needs to see a list of tools to send off to Cloud,

34
00:02:35.919 --> 00:02:40.000
along with the user's query. So it's going to ask the MCP client to get a list of tools.

35
00:02:40.559 --> 00:02:45.520
The MCP client in turn is going to send a list tools request off to the server,

36
00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:51.199
and the server will respond with a list tools result. Now that our MCP client has a list of

37
00:02:51.199 --> 00:02:56.080
the tools, it will give that list of tools back to the server. And now our server has everything

38
00:02:56.080 --> 00:03:01.360
it needs to make an initial request off to Cloud. It has both the original message from the user

39
00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:06.559
and a list of tools to include. So our server can make a request off to Cloud with that query

40
00:03:06.559 --> 00:03:11.119
and the set of tools. Cloud is going to take a look at the tools and realize, you know what,

41
00:03:11.119 --> 00:03:16.720
in order to answer the user's original question right here, I really want to call a tool. So

42
00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:22.479
Cloud would respond with some tool use message part. At this point, our server is going to realize

43
00:03:22.479 --> 00:03:28.080
that Cloud wants to run a tool, but our server is no longer really in charge of executing any

44
00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:34.080
tools. Instead, our tools are going to be executed by the MCP server. So in order to run the tool

45
00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:39.520
that Cloud is asking for, our server is going to ask the MCP client to run a tool with some

46
00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:44.240
particular arguments that are provided by Cloud. The MCP client, however, doesn't actually run

47
00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:50.720
the tool. It's going to send a call tool request off to the MCP server. The MCP server will receive

48
00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:56.000
that request and make a follow-up request off to GitHub. So this is where we would actually be

49
00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:01.919
getting a list of repositories that belong to this particular user. GitHub would respond with that

50
00:04:01.919 --> 00:04:08.639
list of repositories. Then the MCP server would wrap up that data inside of a call tool result

51
00:04:08.639 --> 00:04:14.320
and send that back to the MCP client. Then the MCP client in turn would hand the result off to

52
00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:20.799
our server. Now our server has the list of repositories and it can make a follow-up request

53
00:04:20.799 --> 00:04:26.799
to Cloud with the tool result parked inside of a user message. So this tool result would include

54
00:04:26.799 --> 00:04:32.160
the list of repositories that Cloud was asking for. And now Cloud has all the information it

55
00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:36.480
needs to formulate a final response. So it'll write out some text of something like your

56
00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:40.880
repositories are and then send that back to our server. And our server would send it on back to

57
00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:47.040
our user. All right, so this flow, yes, it is rather complicated. The reason I want to show

58
00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:51.519
you this is that we are going to see all these different pieces as you and I start to implement

59
00:04:51.519 --> 00:04:56.160
our own custom MCP client and MCP server a little bit later on.